{"title": "12. Waqf and Ibtidāʾ – Stopping and Starting", "intro": "Stopping in the right place preserves meaning. The mushaf uses stop signs, and the way a word is pronounced changes at a stop.", "rules": [
 {"name": "Stop signs in the Madinah mushaf", "arabic": "علامات الوقف",
  "text": "مـ = compulsory stop; لا = do not stop; ج = permissible stop; صلى = continuing is preferred; قلى = stopping is preferred; three dots written twice = stop at one of the two places, not both (muʿānaqah); a small sīn-shaped mark = sakt. Note that لا on a verse end does not forbid stopping at the end of the verse.",
  "examples": [(2, 2, ["ريب", "فيه"]), (2, 255, ["القيوم"]), (18, 1, ["عوجا"]), (83, 14, ["كلا"])]},
 {"name": "How a word changes at a stop", "arabic": "كيفية الوقف",
  "text": "Stopping is done with sukūn on the last letter. Tāʾ marbūṭah (ة) becomes هـ. Tanwīn fatḥ becomes an alif of two counts (madd ʿiwaḍ). Tanwīn ḍamm and kasr drop entirely. A final madd letter is kept at 2, 4 or 6 counts. Rawm (a faint vowel) and ishmām (lip rounding without sound) are permitted for ḍammah/kasrah when stopping, but not for fatḥah.",
  "examples": [(1, 3, ["الرحيم"]), (110, 2, ["أفواجا"]), (112, 2, ["الصمد"]), (2, 3, ["الصلوة"])]},
 {"name": "The two-count 'wasted' alif of tanwīn vs. tāʾ marbūṭah", "arabic": "الوقف على التنوين والتاء المربوطة",
  "text": "رَحْمَةً is stopped upon as رَحْمَهْ (no alif), whereas مَاءً becomes مَاءَا. The mushaf's Uthmani spelling makes the difference visible.",
  "examples": [(28, 43, ["ورحمة"]), (78, 14, ["ماء", "ثجاجا"])]},
]},
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
{"title": "13. Rules Specific to the Riwāyah of Ḥafṣ", "intro": "These are the well-known places where Ḥafṣ (via the Shāṭibiyyah) differs from the general pattern, each marked in the Madinah mushaf.", "rules": [
 {"name": "Sakt (breathless pause) – four obligatory places", "arabic": "السكتات الأربع",
  "text": "A brief pause without taking a breath, marked ۜ: (1) 18:1–2 on عِوَجًا before قَيِّمًا; (2) 36:52 on مَّرْقَدِنَا before هَٰذَا; (3) 75:27 on مَنْ before رَاقٍ; (4) 83:14 on بَلْ before رَانَ. The sakt prevents idghām/iqlāb across the pause. Optional sakt: 69:28–29 between مَالِيَهْ and هَلَكَ (or idghām of the two hāʾs), and between al-Anfāl and al-Tawbah.",
  "examples": [(18, 1, ["عوجا"]), (18, 2, ["قيما"]), (36, 52, ["مرقدنا", "هذا"]), (75, 27, ["من", "راق"]), (83, 14, ["بل", "ران"]), (69, 28, ["ماليه"]), (69, 29, ["هلك"])]},
 {"name": "Imālah – one place", "arabic": "الإمالة",
  "text": "Ḥafṣ has imālah (leaning the fatḥah/alif toward kasrah/yāʾ, like a long 'ee' sound) in one word only: مَجْر۪ىٰهَا (11:41). Marked with a small rhombus (۪) under the rāʾ.",
  "examples": [(11, 41, ["مجرىها"])]},
 {"name": "Tas-hīl – softening the hamzah", "arabic": "التسهيل",
  "text": "In ءَا۬عْجَمِىٌّ (41:44) the second hamzah is obligatorily softened (pronounced between hamzah and alif), marked with a small circle-dot (۬). Tas-hīl is also one of the two wajhs in ءَآلْـَٰٔنَ, ءَآللَّهُ and ءَآلذَّكَرَيْنِ (see madd farq).",
  "examples": [(41, 44, ["ءاعجمي"]), (10, 51, ["ءالن"])]},
 {"name": "Ishmām and Ikhtilās in تَأْمَنَّا", "arabic": "الإشمام والاختلاس",
  "text": "In لَا تَأْمَ۫نَّا (12:11) the original is تَأْمَنُنَا. Ḥafṣ reads either with ishmām (rounding the lips toward a ḍammah during the ghunnah of the merged nūn, without any sound) or with ikhtilās/rawm (pronouncing two-thirds of the ḍammah quickly, in which case there is no idghām). Marked ۫ in the mushaf.",
  "examples": [(12, 11, ["تأمنا"])]},
 {"name": "Sīn written as ṣād", "arabic": "الصاد التي تقرأ سيناً",
  "text": "Four words are written with ص but a small س (ۜ / ۣ) above tells the reader which to pronounce: يَبْصُۜطُ (2:245) – read with sīn; بَصْۜطَةً (7:69) – read with sīn; ٱلْمُصَۣيْطِرُونَ (52:37) – Ḥafṣ may read sīn or ṣād (ṣād preferred by the Shāṭibiyyah); بِمُصَيْطِرٍ (88:22) – read with ṣād only.",
  "examples": [(2, 245, ["ويبصط"]), (7, 69, ["بصطة"]), (52, 37, ["المصيطرون"]), (88, 22, ["بمصيطر"])]},
 {"name": "ضَعْف in Sūrat al-Rūm", "arabic": "ضعف في سورة الروم",
  "text": "In 30:54 the word ضَعْف occurs three times. Ḥafṣ transmits both fatḥah (ضَعْف) and ḍammah (ضُعْف) on the ḍād; the fatḥah is the preferred wajh by the Shāṭibiyyah path.",
  "examples": [(30, 54, ["ضعف"])]},
 {"name": "The mīm of الٓمٓ joined to ٱللَّهُ", "arabic": "ميم الم آل عمران",
  "text": "At the start of Āl ʿImrān (3:1–2), when الٓمٓ is joined to ٱللَّهُ, the final mīm takes a fatḥah for the hamzat al-waṣl. Ḥafṣ may then lengthen the mīm 6 counts (keeping the lāzim madd) or shorten it to 2 counts (because the sukūn is no longer present).",
  "examples": [(3, 1, ["الم"]), (3, 2, ["الله"])]},
 {"name": "Other Ḥafṣ specifics", "arabic": "مسائل أخرى",
  "text": "Ḥafṣ reads كُفُوًا (112:4) and هُزُوًا (2:67 and elsewhere) with a wāw carrying ḍammah before it and no hamzah, where other readers have كُفْئًا / هُزْءًا. Ḥafṣ has no idghām kabīr (merging of a voweled letter into the next) — every idghām in this riwāyah involves a sākin first letter.",
  "examples": [(112, 4, ["كفوا"]), (2, 67, ["هزوا"])]},
]},
